19 < 1 ### Task 1: Summarize a ledger in one line
20 < 1 
21 < 1 **Type:** implementation
22 < 1 
23 < 1 **Files:**
24 < 1 - Modify: `ledger/core.py`
25 < 1 
26 < 1 **Claim:** An operator reads how many entries a ledger holds and what they sum to, in one line. (derived)
27 < 1 Machine: M1. `summary("2026-09-01,cash,-12.50,coffee\n2026-09-02,card,900,rent, deposit\n")` is exactly `"2 entries, total 887.50"`.
28 < 1 
29 < 1 **Authorized-by:** operator's pick, 2026-11-13
30 < 1 
31 < 1 **Interfaces:**
32 < 1 - Consumes: nothing
33 < 1 - Produces: `summary(text: str) -> str`
34 < 1 
35 < 1 **Context:** `summary(text)` loads the text with the module's `load(text)` and answers the number of entries, the word `entries`, a comma, the word `total` and `dollars` of the module's `total(entries)`.
36 < 1 
37 < 1 **Proof:**
38 < 1 - Run: python3 -c "from ledger.core import summary; assert summary('2026-09-01,cash,-12.50,coffee\n2026-09-02,card,900,rent, deposit\n') == '2 entries, total 887.50'" [M1]
39 < 1 - Legs: (a) the one-line summary of a two-entry ledger, byte for byte [M1].
